Here is our payment method hierarchy. Gift cards were added last month and refunds started failing in production. What does SOLID say about this design, and how would you fix it?

```typescript
abstract class PaymentMethod {
  abstract charge(amountCents: number): Promise<string>;
  abstract refund(chargeId: string, amountCents: number): Promise<void>;
  abstract saveForLater(customerId: string): Promise<void>;
}

class CardPayment extends PaymentMethod {
  async charge(amountCents: number) { return processor.charge(this.token, amountCents); }
  async refund(chargeId: string, amountCents: number) { await processor.refund(chargeId, amountCents); }
  async saveForLater(customerId: string) { await vault.store(customerId, this.token); }
  constructor(private token: string) { super(); }
}

class GiftCardPayment extends PaymentMethod {
  constructor(private code: string) { super(); }
  async charge(amountCents: number) {
    const balance = await giftCards.balance(this.code);
    if (balance < amountCents) throw new Error('Insufficient balance');
    return giftCards.debit(this.code, amountCents);
  }
  async refund() { throw new Error('Gift cards cannot be refunded'); }
  async saveForLater() { /* not supported */ }
}

async function cancelOrder(order: Order, method: PaymentMethod) {
  await method.refund(order.chargeId, order.totalCents);
  order.status = 'cancelled';
  await orders.save(order);
}
```

### What SOLID says

#### Liskov Substitution Principle — violated (this is your production bug)
`cancelOrder` is written against `PaymentMethod` and relies on its contract: `refund()` returns money. `GiftCardPayment.refund()` throws instead, so substituting a gift card for a card breaks the caller. The order is never marked `cancelled`, and because the throw happens before `orders.save`, the customer sees an error with no way to cancel.

`saveForLater()` is a quieter LSP violation: it silently does nothing. A checkout flow that calls it and then shows "Card saved" lies to the user. A subtype that weakens a postcondition (no-op) is as broken as one that throws; it just fails later.

#### Interface Segregation Principle — violated
The base class forces every method to support charge, refund and storage. Gift cards only really support charging (and crediting back a balance). The fat abstraction is what pushes subclasses into throwing or no-op stubs.

#### Not the main issue: Open/Closed
Adding `GiftCardPayment` did not require editing `CardPayment`, so the hierarchy is open for extension. The problem is that the new subtype cannot honour the old contract, which is an LSP problem, not an OCP one.

#### Also worth fixing
`charge()` throws `'Insufficient balance'`, a normal business outcome, so callers must wrap every charge in `try/catch` and parse messages.

---

### Refactoring

Split the capabilities into small interfaces and model "refund the payment" as something each method answers honestly. A gift card's refund is a balance credit, which is a real refund for the customer.

```typescript
export type PaymentError =
  | { kind: 'InsufficientBalance'; availableCents: number }
  | { kind: 'Declined'; reason: string };

export interface Chargeable {
  charge(amountCents: number): Promise<Result<string, PaymentError>>;
}

export interface Refundable {
  refund(chargeId: string, amountCents: number): Promise<Result<void, PaymentError>>;
}

export interface Storable {
  saveForLater(customerId: string): Promise<void>;
}

export interface CardProcessor {
  charge(token: string, amountCents: number): Promise<Result<string, PaymentError>>;
  refund(chargeId: string, amountCents: number): Promise<Result<void, PaymentError>>;
}

export interface CardVault {
  store(customerId: string, token: string): Promise<void>;
}

export interface GiftCardLedger {
  balance(code: string): Promise<number>;
  debit(code: string, amountCents: number): Promise<string>;
  credit(code: string, amountCents: number): Promise<void>;
}

export class CardPayment implements Chargeable, Refundable, Storable {
  constructor(private readonly token: string, private readonly processor: CardProcessor, private readonly vault: CardVault) {}

  charge(amountCents: number) { return this.processor.charge(this.token, amountCents); }
  refund(chargeId: string, amountCents: number) { return this.processor.refund(chargeId, amountCents); }
  saveForLater(customerId: string) { return this.vault.store(customerId, this.token); }
}

export class GiftCardPayment implements Chargeable, Refundable {
  constructor(private readonly code: string, private readonly ledger: GiftCardLedger) {}

  async charge(amountCents: number): Promise<Result<string, PaymentError>> {
    const available = await this.ledger.balance(this.code);
    if (available < amountCents) return Result.fail({ kind: 'InsufficientBalance', availableCents: available });
    return Result.ok(await this.ledger.debit(this.code, amountCents));
  }

  async refund(_chargeId: string, amountCents: number): Promise<Result<void, PaymentError>> {
    await this.ledger.credit(this.code, amountCents);
    return Result.ok(undefined);
  }
}
```

`GiftCardPayment` no longer claims `Storable`, so the compiler stops anyone from offering "save this gift card". `cancelOrder` asks only for what it needs:

```typescript
export interface CancellableOrder {
  readonly chargeId: string;
  readonly totalCents: number;
  status: 'paid' | 'cancelled';
}

export interface OrderStore {
  save(order: CancellableOrder): Promise<void>;
}

export async function cancelOrder(
  order: CancellableOrder,
  method: Refundable,
  store: OrderStore,
): Promise<Result<void, PaymentError>> {
  const refunded = await method.refund(order.chargeId, order.totalCents);
  if (!refunded.success) return refunded;
  order.status = 'cancelled';
  await store.save(order);
  return Result.ok(undefined);
}
```

If a future method genuinely cannot be refunded (say, a cash-on-delivery voucher), it simply does not implement `Refundable`, and the code that builds the cancellation flow must decide what to do with it at compile time instead of discovering it in production.

#### 1. Port Interface (`src/application/ports/ITokenService.ts`)
```typescript
export interface TokenPayload {
  readonly accountId: string;
  readonly role: string;
}

export interface ITokenService {
  generateToken(payload: TokenPayload): string;
  verifyToken(token: string): TokenPayload | null;
}
```

#### 2. Concrete Adapter (`src/infrastructure/services/JwtTokenService.ts`)
```typescript
import jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';
import { ITokenService, TokenPayload } from '../../application/ports/ITokenService';

export class JwtTokenService implements ITokenService {
  constructor(
    private readonly secret: string,
    private readonly expiresIn: string = '1h'
  ) {}

  generateToken(payload: TokenPayload): string {
    return jwt.sign(payload, this.secret, { expiresIn: this.expiresIn });
  }

  verifyToken(token: string): TokenPayload | null {
    try {
      const decoded = jwt.verify(token, this.secret) as jwt.JwtPayload;
      return {
        accountId: decoded.accountId as string,
        role: decoded.role as string,
      };
    } catch {
      return null;
    }
  }
}
```

### Clean Architecture Inversion:
- The application layer dictates the contract (`ITokenService`).
- The infrastructure layer implements it using `jsonwebtoken`.
- Swapping to PASETO, Jose, or Web Crypto API requires zero changes in application use cases.

```typescript
export class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';
  }
}

export class EmailAddress {
  private readonly value: string;
  private static readonly EMAIL_REGEX = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;

  private constructor(email: string) {
    this.value = email;
  }

  public static create(rawEmail: string): EmailAddress {
    if (!rawEmail || rawEmail.trim().length === 0) {
      throw new ValidationError('email address cannot be empty');
    }
    const trimmed = rawEmail.trim().toLowerCase();
    if (!this.EMAIL_REGEX.test(trimmed)) {
      throw new ValidationError(`invalid email address format: ${rawEmail}`);
    }
    return new EmailAddress(trimmed);
  }

  public getValue(): string {
    return this.value;
  }

  public equals(other: EmailAddress): boolean {
    return this.value === other.value;
  }
}
```

### Why this conforms to Clean Architecture:
1. **Typed Domain Error:** Throws `ValidationError` (a typed domain error) instead of a generic `Error`, making the contract explicit for consumers.
2. **Zero External Dependencies:** Relies strictly on standard TypeScript and ECMAScript features; no `validator.js` or `zod` polluting the core domain.
3. **Immutability:** The inner value cannot be altered after creation.
4. **Always Valid:** An instance of `EmailAddress` cannot exist in an invalid state.

Test `EnrollInCourse` in Arrange-Act-Assert form. Fake the catalog and the transcript in memory; I especially want to see the case where only some prerequisites are passed.

```typescript
import { Result } from '../shared/Result';

export interface Course {
  code: string;
  prerequisites: string[];
  capacity: number;
  enrolled: string[];
}

export interface CourseCatalog {
  find(code: string): Promise<Course | null>;
  save(course: Course): Promise<void>;
}

export interface Transcript {
  passedCourses(studentId: string): Promise<string[]>;
}

export type EnrollError =
  | { kind: 'CourseNotFound' }
  | { kind: 'AlreadyEnrolled' }
  | { kind: 'MissingPrerequisites'; missing: string[] }
  | { kind: 'CourseFull' };

export class EnrollInCourse {
  constructor(private readonly catalog: CourseCatalog, private readonly transcript: Transcript) {}

  async execute(studentId: string, code: string): Promise<Result<void, EnrollError>> {
    const course = await this.catalog.find(code);
    if (!course) return Result.fail({ kind: 'CourseNotFound' });
    if (course.enrolled.includes(studentId)) return Result.fail({ kind: 'AlreadyEnrolled' });
    const passed = new Set(await this.transcript.passedCourses(studentId));
    const missing = course.prerequisites.filter((p) => !passed.has(p));
    if (missing.length > 0) return Result.fail({ kind: 'MissingPrerequisites', missing });
    if (course.enrolled.length >= course.capacity) return Result.fail({ kind: 'CourseFull' });
    await this.catalog.save({ ...course, enrolled: [...course.enrolled, studentId] });
    return Result.ok(undefined);
  }
}
```

This use case has no time dependency, so no clock is needed. Both collaborators are in-memory fakes; the transcript fake is seeded per student, and the catalog fake stores courses by code so each test can read the roster after `execute`.

```typescript
import { describe, it, expect, beforeEach } from 'vitest';
import { EnrollInCourse, Course, CourseCatalog, Transcript } from './EnrollInCourse';

class InMemoryCatalog implements CourseCatalog {
  readonly courses = new Map<string, Course>();
  async find(code: string): Promise<Course | null> {
    return this.courses.get(code) ?? null;
  }
  async save(course: Course): Promise<void> {
    this.courses.set(course.code, course);
  }
}

class InMemoryTranscript implements Transcript {
  private readonly passed = new Map<string, string[]>();
  pass(studentId: string, ...codes: string[]): void {
    this.passed.set(studentId, [...(this.passed.get(studentId) ?? []), ...codes]);
  }
  async passedCourses(studentId: string): Promise<string[]> {
    return this.passed.get(studentId) ?? [];
  }
}

const algorithms = (overrides: Partial<Course> = {}): Course => ({
  code: 'CS301', prerequisites: ['CS101', 'MATH201'], capacity: 2, enrolled: [], ...overrides,
});

describe('EnrollInCourse', () => {
  let catalog: InMemoryCatalog;
  let transcript: InMemoryTranscript;
  let useCase: EnrollInCourse;

  beforeEach(() => {
    catalog = new InMemoryCatalog();
    transcript = new InMemoryTranscript();
    useCase = new EnrollInCourse(catalog, transcript);
  });

  it('enrolls a student who passed every prerequisite', async () => {
    // Arrange
    catalog.courses.set('CS301', algorithms());
    transcript.pass('s-1', 'CS101', 'MATH201');

    // Act
    const result = await useCase.execute('s-1', 'CS301');

    // Assert
    expect(result.success).toBe(true);
    expect(catalog.courses.get('CS301')?.enrolled).toEqual(['s-1']);
  });

  it('lists only the prerequisites that are still missing', async () => {
    // Arrange
    catalog.courses.set('CS301', algorithms());
    transcript.pass('s-1', 'CS101');

    // Act
    const result = await useCase.execute('s-1', 'CS301');

    // Assert
    expect(result.success).toBe(false);
    if (result.success) return;
    expect(result.error).toEqual({ kind: 'MissingPrerequisites', missing: ['MATH201'] });
    expect(catalog.courses.get('CS301')?.enrolled).toEqual([]);
  });

  it('fails with CourseNotFound for an unknown code', async () => {
    // Arrange: empty catalog

    // Act
    const result = await useCase.execute('s-1', 'CS999');

    // Assert
    expect(result.success).toBe(false);
    if (result.success) return;
    expect(result.error.kind).toBe('CourseNotFound');
  });

  it('fails with AlreadyEnrolled when the student is on the roster', async () => {
    // Arrange
    catalog.courses.set('CS301', algorithms({ enrolled: ['s-1'] }));

    // Act
    const result = await useCase.execute('s-1', 'CS301');

    // Assert
    expect(result.success).toBe(false);
    if (result.success) return;
    expect(result.error.kind).toBe('AlreadyEnrolled');
  });

  it('fails with CourseFull when capacity is reached', async () => {
    // Arrange
    catalog.courses.set('CS301', algorithms({ enrolled: ['s-8', 's-9'] }));
    transcript.pass('s-1', 'CS101', 'MATH201');

    // Act
    const result = await useCase.execute('s-1', 'CS301');

    // Assert
    expect(result.success).toBe(false);
    if (result.success) return;
    expect(result.error.kind).toBe('CourseFull');
    expect(catalog.courses.get('CS301')?.enrolled).toHaveLength(2);
  });
});
```

```typescript
// Domain/Errors/ValidationError.ts
export class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "ValidationError";
  }
}

// Domain/ValueObjects/Email.ts
import { Result, ok, err } from "../Result";
import { ValidationError } from "../Errors/ValidationError";

export class Email {
  private constructor(private readonly value: string) {
    // Private constructor to enforce creation through static factory method
  }

  /**
   * Creates a new Email value object with validation
   * @param email The email string to validate and normalize
   * @returns Result<Email, ValidationError> - Success with Email value object or error
   */
  static create(email: string): Result<Email, ValidationError> {
    // Check if email is empty or null
    if (!email || email.trim() === "") {
      return err(new ValidationError("Email cannot be empty"));
    }

    // Normalize to lowercase
    const normalizedEmail = email.toLowerCase().trim();

    // Validate email format using regex
    const emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
    if (!emailRegex.test(normalizedEmail)) {
      return err(new ValidationError("Invalid email format"));
    }

    // Additional validation: ensure no consecutive dots
    if (normalizedEmail.includes("..")) {
      return err(new ValidationError("Email cannot contain consecutive dots"));
    }

    // Additional validation: ensure no leading/trailing dots
    if (normalizedEmail.startsWith(".") || normalizedEmail.endsWith(".")) {
      return err(new ValidationError("Email cannot start or end with a dot"));
    }

    return ok(new Email(normalizedEmail));
  }

  /**
   * Gets the normalized email value
   */
  getValue(): string {
    return this.value;
  }

  /**
   * Checks if two emails are equal
   */
  equals(other: Email): boolean {
    return this.value === other.value;
  }

  /**
   * Returns string representation of the email
   */
  toString(): string {
    return this.value;
  }
}

// Domain/Result.ts
export type Result<T, E> = Success<T> | Failure<E>;

class Success<T> {
  constructor(public readonly value: T) {}
  isSuccess = true as const;
  isFailure = false as const;
}

class Failure<E> {
  constructor(public readonly error: E) {}
  isSuccess = false as const;
  isFailure = true as const;
}

export function ok<T>(value: T): Result<T, never> {
  return new Success(value);
}

export function err<E>(error: E): Result<never, E> {
  return new Failure(error);
}
```

This implementation provides:

1. **Pure Value Object**: The `Email` class is immutable and represents a single email value
2. **Invariant Validation**:
   - Non-empty validation
   - Email format validation using regex
   - Lowercase normalization
   - Additional validations for edge cases (consecutive dots, leading/trailing dots)
3. **Result Pattern**: Returns `Result<Email, ValidationError>` for safe error handling
4. **Dependency Rule Compliance**: 
   - Domain layer doesn't depend on external libraries
   - No framework or infrastructure dependencies
5. **Typed Errors**: Uses `ValidationError` for specific error handling
6. **Safe Construction**: Uses static factory method `create()` to enforce validation
7. **Utility Methods**: Includes `equals()`, `getValue()`, and `toString()` for practical use

The implementation follows Clean Architecture principles by keeping domain logic independent of external concerns while providing a robust, validated email value object.

Critique this notifier with the Open/Closed Principle and SOLID and refactor it:

```typescript
class NotificationSender {
  send(channel: string, to: string, text: string) {
    if (channel === 'sms') {
      smsClient.send(to, text);
    } else if (channel === 'push') {
      pushClient.send(to, text);
    } else if (channel === 'slack') {
      slackClient.post(to, text);
    }
  }
}
```

### SOLID Critique
- **OCP (violated):** each new channel edits `send`, touching code that already works.
- **DIP (violated):** the class depends directly on three concrete SDK clients.
- **Silent failure:** an unknown channel does nothing instead of failing loudly.

---

### Refactoring: channel abstraction plus registry
```typescript
export interface NotificationChannel {
  send(to: string, text: string): Promise<void>;
}

export class SmsChannel implements NotificationChannel {
  constructor(private readonly client: { send(to: string, text: string): Promise<void> }) {}
  send(to: string, text: string): Promise<void> { return this.client.send(to, text); }
}

export class SlackChannel implements NotificationChannel {
  constructor(private readonly client: { post(to: string, text: string): Promise<void> }) {}
  send(to: string, text: string): Promise<void> { return this.client.post(to, text); }
}

export class NotificationSender {
  constructor(private readonly channels: ReadonlyMap<string, NotificationChannel>) {}

  async send(channel: string, to: string, text: string): Promise<void> {
    const target = this.channels.get(channel);
    if (!target) throw new Error(`Unsupported channel: ${channel}`);
    await target.send(to, text);
  }
}
```

A new channel is one new class registered in the composition root. `NotificationSender` never changes (OCP), depends only on the abstraction (DIP), and fails explicitly on unknown channels.

Refactor the following javascript code to adhere strictly to Clean Architecture and Ports & Adapters:

```javascript
const db = require('../db');
const mailer = require('../mailer');

async function requestLeave(employeeId, start, end, type) {
  const employee = await db.employees.findById(employeeId);
  const from = new Date(start);
  const to = new Date(end);
  if (to < from) throw new Error('End before start');
  let days = 0;
  for (let d = new Date(from); d <= to; d.setDate(d.getDate() + 1)) {
    if (d.getDay() !== 0 && d.getDay() !== 6) days++;
  }
  if (type === 'vacation' && days > employee.vacationBalance) throw new Error('Not enough balance');
  if (type === 'sick' && days > 3 && !employee.hasMedicalNote) throw new Error('Medical note required');
  const leave = await db.leaves.insert({ employeeId, start: from, end: to, type, days, status: 'pending' });
  await mailer.send(employee.managerEmail, 'Leave request', `${employee.name} requested ${days} day(s)`);
  return leave;
}

module.exports = { requestLeave };

```

### Architectural Flaws in Original Code
1. **HR policy mixed with I/O:** working-day counting, vacation balance and the sick-leave note rule share a function with database and mail calls.
2. **Module-level singletons:** `require('../db')` and `require('../mailer')` cannot be replaced in tests.
3. **Thrown strings:** callers must parse error messages to know why a request failed.
4. **Missing employee is not handled:** `employee` may be `undefined`.

---

### Clean Architecture Refactoring (TypeScript)

#### 1. Domain Layer (`src/domain/leave.ts`)
```typescript
import { Result } from '../shared/Result';

export type LeaveType = 'vacation' | 'sick' | 'unpaid';

export type LeaveError =
  | { kind: 'EmployeeNotFound' }
  | { kind: 'InvalidRange' }
  | { kind: 'InsufficientBalance'; requested: number; available: number }
  | { kind: 'MedicalNoteRequired' };

export interface Employee {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly name: string;
  readonly managerContact: string;
  readonly vacationBalance: number;
  readonly hasMedicalNote: boolean;
}

export interface LeaveRequest {
  readonly employeeId: string;
  readonly start: Date;
  readonly end: Date;
  readonly type: LeaveType;
  readonly days: number;
  readonly status: 'pending';
}

export const SICK_DAYS_WITHOUT_NOTE = 3;

export function workingDays(from: Date, to: Date): number {
  let days = 0;
  for (const d = new Date(from); d <= to; d.setDate(d.getDate() + 1)) {
    if (d.getDay() !== 0 && d.getDay() !== 6) days++;
  }
  return days;
}

export function requestLeave(
  employee: Employee, start: Date, end: Date, type: LeaveType,
): Result<LeaveRequest, LeaveError> {
  if (end < start) return Result.fail({ kind: 'InvalidRange' });
  const days = workingDays(start, end);
  if (type === 'vacation' && days > employee.vacationBalance) {
    return Result.fail({ kind: 'InsufficientBalance', requested: days, available: employee.vacationBalance });
  }
  if (type === 'sick' && days > SICK_DAYS_WITHOUT_NOTE && !employee.hasMedicalNote) {
    return Result.fail({ kind: 'MedicalNoteRequired' });
  }
  return Result.ok({ employeeId: employee.id, start, end, type, days, status: 'pending' });
}
```

#### 2. Application Layer (`src/application/SubmitLeaveRequest.ts`)
```typescript
import { Result } from '../shared/Result';
import { Employee, LeaveError, LeaveRequest, LeaveType, requestLeave } from '../domain/leave';

export interface EmployeeDirectory { findById(id: string): Promise<Employee | null>; }
export interface LeaveRepository { add(request: LeaveRequest): Promise<LeaveRequest>; }
export interface ManagerNotifier { leaveRequested(manager: string, employeeName: string, days: number): Promise<void>; }

export class SubmitLeaveRequest {
  constructor(
    private readonly employees: EmployeeDirectory,
    private readonly leaves: LeaveRepository,
    private readonly notifier: ManagerNotifier,
  ) {}

  async execute(input: { employeeId: string; start: Date; end: Date; type: LeaveType }): Promise<Result<LeaveRequest, LeaveError>> {
    const employee = await this.employees.findById(input.employeeId);
    if (!employee) return Result.fail({ kind: 'EmployeeNotFound' });

    const request = requestLeave(employee, input.start, input.end, input.type);
    if (!request.success) return request;

    const saved = await this.leaves.add(request.data);
    await this.notifier.leaveRequested(employee.managerContact, employee.name, saved.days);
    return Result.ok(saved);
  }
}
```

#### 3. Infrastructure Layer (`src/infrastructure/leaveAdapters.ts`)
The existing `db` and `mailer` modules are wrapped instead of imported by the business code.
```typescript
import { LeaveRequest } from '../domain/leave';
import { LeaveRepository, ManagerNotifier } from '../application/SubmitLeaveRequest';

interface LeavesTable { insert(row: LeaveRequest): Promise<LeaveRequest>; }
interface Mailer { send(to: string, subject: string, body: string): Promise<void>; }

export class DbLeaveRepository implements LeaveRepository {
  constructor(private readonly leaves: LeavesTable) {}
  add(request: LeaveRequest): Promise<LeaveRequest> { return this.leaves.insert(request); }
}

export class MailManagerNotifier implements ManagerNotifier {
  constructor(private readonly mailer: Mailer) {}
  async leaveRequested(manager: string, employeeName: string, days: number): Promise<void> {
    await this.mailer.send(manager, 'Leave request', `${employeeName} requested ${days} day(s)`);
  }
}
```
A `DbEmployeeDirectory` maps `managerEmail` to `managerContact` the same way.

Refactor this concert ticketing route. Seat hold rules are implemented with raw Redis commands inside Express:

```typescript
app.post('/shows/:showId/holds', async (req, res) => {
  const { seats, sessionId } = req.body as { seats: string[]; sessionId: string };
  if (seats.length === 0 || seats.length > 8) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Select 1 to 8 seats' });
  const existing = await redis.keys(`hold:${req.params.showId}:*:${sessionId}`);
  if (existing.length + seats.length > 8) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Max 8 seats per session' });
  const sold = await redis.smembers(`sold:${req.params.showId}`);
  if (seats.some((s) => sold.includes(s))) return res.status(409).json({ error: 'Seat already sold' });
  const held: string[] = [];
  for (const seat of seats) {
    const ok = await redis.set(`hold:${req.params.showId}:${seat}:${sessionId}`, '1', 'EX', 600, 'NX');
    const other = await redis.keys(`hold:${req.params.showId}:${seat}:*`);
    if (!ok || other.length > 1) {
      for (const h of held) await redis.del(`hold:${req.params.showId}:${h}:${sessionId}`);
      return res.status(409).json({ error: `Seat ${seat} is held by someone else` });
    }
    held.push(seat);
  }
  res.status(201).json({ seats: held, expiresInSeconds: 600 });
});
```

### Architectural Flaws in Original Code
1. **Booking policy in Redis commands:** the 8-seat limit and 10-minute hold live in Express code.
2. **`KEYS` in a request path** scans the whole keyspace and is not safe in production.
3. **Race-prone holds:** a hold is set first and checked for conflicts afterwards.

---

### Clean Architecture Refactoring

#### 1. Domain Layer (`src/domain/holds.ts`)
```typescript
import { Result } from '../shared/Result';

export const MAX_SEATS_PER_SESSION = 8;
export const HOLD_SECONDS = 600;

export type HoldError =
  | { kind: 'InvalidSelection' }
  | { kind: 'SessionLimit'; max: number }
  | { kind: 'SeatSold'; seat: string }
  | { kind: 'SeatHeld'; seat: string };

export function checkSelection(
  requested: readonly string[], alreadyHeld: number, sold: ReadonlySet<string>,
): Result<readonly string[], HoldError> {
  if (requested.length === 0 || requested.length > MAX_SEATS_PER_SESSION) return Result.fail({ kind: 'InvalidSelection' });
  if (alreadyHeld + requested.length > MAX_SEATS_PER_SESSION) {
    return Result.fail({ kind: 'SessionLimit', max: MAX_SEATS_PER_SESSION });
  }
  const soldSeat = requested.find((s) => sold.has(s));
  if (soldSeat) return Result.fail({ kind: 'SeatSold', seat: soldSeat });
  return Result.ok(requested);
}
```

#### 2. Application Layer (`src/application/HoldSeats.ts`)
```typescript
import { Result } from '../shared/Result';
import { HOLD_SECONDS, HoldError, checkSelection } from '../domain/holds';

export interface SeatInventory {
  soldSeats(showId: string): Promise<Set<string>>;
  heldBySession(showId: string, sessionId: string): Promise<number>;
  /** Holds all seats atomically, or none; returns the first seat that was already held. */
  holdAll(showId: string, sessionId: string, seats: readonly string[], ttlSeconds: number): Promise<string | null>;
}

export class HoldSeats {
  constructor(private readonly inventory: SeatInventory) {}

  async execute(showId: string, sessionId: string, seats: readonly string[]): Promise<Result<{ seats: readonly string[]; expiresInSeconds: number }, HoldError>> {
    const [held, sold] = await Promise.all([
      this.inventory.heldBySession(showId, sessionId),
      this.inventory.soldSeats(showId),
    ]);
    const selection = checkSelection(seats, held, sold);
    if (!selection.success) return selection;

    const conflict = await this.inventory.holdAll(showId, sessionId, selection.data, HOLD_SECONDS);
    if (conflict) return Result.fail({ kind: 'SeatHeld', seat: conflict });
    return Result.ok({ seats: selection.data, expiresInSeconds: HOLD_SECONDS });
  }
}
```

#### 3. Infrastructure Layer (`src/infrastructure/RedisSeatInventory.ts`)
```typescript
import type { Redis } from 'ioredis';
import { SeatInventory } from '../application/HoldSeats';

// Seat keys (value = session) + a per-session sorted set scored by expiry, updated in one Lua script.
const HOLD_ALL = `
local session, ttl, now = ARGV[1], tonumber(ARGV[2]), tonumber(ARGV[3])
for i = 2, #KEYS do
  local owner = redis.call('GET', KEYS[i])
  if owner and owner ~= session then return ARGV[i + 2] end
end
for i = 2, #KEYS do
  redis.call('SET', KEYS[i], session, 'EX', ttl)
  redis.call('ZADD', KEYS[1], now + ttl * 1000, ARGV[i + 2])
end
redis.call('ZREMRANGEBYSCORE', KEYS[1], '-inf', now)
redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], ttl * 1000)
return false`;

export class RedisSeatInventory implements SeatInventory {
  constructor(private readonly redis: Redis) {}

  async soldSeats(showId: string): Promise<Set<string>> {
    return new Set(await this.redis.smembers(`sold:${showId}`));
  }

  async heldBySession(showId: string, sessionId: string): Promise<number> {
    return this.redis.zcount(`session-holds:${showId}:${sessionId}`, `(${Date.now()}`, '+inf');
  }

  async holdAll(showId: string, sessionId: string, seats: readonly string[], ttl: number): Promise<string | null> {
    const keys = [`session-holds:${showId}:${sessionId}`, ...seats.map((s) => `hold:${showId}:${s}`)];
    const conflict = await this.redis.eval(HOLD_ALL, keys.length, ...keys, sessionId, ttl, Date.now(), ...seats);
    return typeof conflict === 'string' ? conflict : null;
  }
}
```

